 7. 🍼 Axios interview: New fight for maternal health 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Olivia Walton speaks last week at the Heartland Summit in Bentonville, Ark. Photo: Michael Connor for Heartland Forward/Heartland Summit BENTONVILLE, Ark. — Olivia Walton , who's leading a $100 million, bipartisan effort to cut the U.S. maternal mortality rate in half by 2031, tells me maternal health is a solvable problem, with state governments "in the driver's seat."

 "We have the medicine," Walton told me last week on the sidelines of the annual Heartland Summit of the nonprofit Heartland Forward , which promotes economic development in America's geographic middle. "We know what works. There are poorer and more rural countries around the world that are doing this better than we are. We also have pockets of what's working here in the United States." Why it matters: Walton and her husband, Tom Walton , a Walmart heir, last month announced an initial $100 million, five-year commitment to Healthy Moms, Healthy Babies America ( HMHBA ) to fund state matching grants, partnerships and infrastructure to scale successful maternal health practices.

 💰 Driving the news: At the summit, Healthy Moms, Healthy Babies announced its first state grants — $10 million each to Arkansas and Kentucky over five years "to accelerate proven approaches to maternal care." 

